==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|ulage}}, from {{der|en|xno|ulliage}}, from {{m|fro|*ullier|t=to fill a partially empty cask}}, from {{der|en|fro|oel|t=bunghole|lit=eye}}, from {{der|en|la|oculus|t=eye}}. See {{m+|fr|ouillage}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈʌl.ɪd͡ʒ/|/ˈʌl.əd͡ʒ/|/ˈjul.ɪd͡ʒ/|/ˈjul.əd͡ʒ/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌlɪdʒ|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# In a wine bottle, the empty space between the cork and the top of the wine.
# In a cask or barrel, the empty space, occupied by air, that is created by not completely filling the cask or barrel, or through spillage.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Excise Officer's Manual and Improved Gauger|author=Joseph Bateman|year=1840|passage=The dry '''ullage''' will be obtained in the same manner, the dry inches being used instead of the wet.}}
# The topping-up of such a barrel with fresh wine.
#: {{syn|en|ulling}}
# In an industrial setting, the empty space in a [[tank]], especially as for [[fuel]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Marine Regulations|page=234|publisher=Esso Shipping Company|year=1950|passage=Upon completion of loading, all cargo tanks shall again be inspected and '''ullages''' carefully measured and recorded, with temperatures, in the Cargo Record Book, Port Log and Supplementary Loading and Discharging Report.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=23 August 2000|author=w:National Transportation Safety Board|title=Aircraft Accident Report: In-flight Breakup Over the Atlantic Ocean, Trans World Airlines Flight 800, Boeing 747-131, N93119, Near East Moriches, New York, July 17, 1996|chapter=1.18.1 Accident Record and History of Fuel Tank Fires/Explosions on Airplanes|url=https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/AccidentReports/Reports/AAR0003.pdf|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220711080450/https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/AccidentReports/Reports/AAR0003.pdf|archivedate=11 July 2022|accessdate=4 August 2022|page=180|text=According to the Colombian Government's report regarding this accident, the bomb explosion itself did not compromise the structural integrity of the airplane; however, the explosion punctured the CWT and ignited the Jet A fuel/air vapor in the '''ullage''', resulting in the destruction of the airplane.}}
# Additional cargo of little or no value taken on to prevent movement of shifting of the purposive cargo.
#* '''1815''' Niles Weekly Register volume 8, Supplement, p. 152. From an inventory of the frigate ''Confiance'':
#*: [&hellip;]5 hhds. rum/ 5 bbls. flour/ 1 bbl. sugar/ 9 do. pork/ 1 do. suett/ 3 do. cocoa/ 6 do. peas/ 2 '''ullage''' bbls. vinegar[&hellip;]
# {{lb|en|slang|obsolete|in the plural}} Left-over wine surreptiously drunk by waiters as they clear away the glasses.<ref>{{cite-text|en|year=1873|author=John Camden Hotten|title=The Slang Dictionary}}</ref>
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1862|journal=Temple Bar: A London Magazine for Town and Country Readers|page=13
|passage=Mopps {{...}} was draining his vast array of wine-glasses, lest the waiters carrying them off should find any "'''ullages'''" in them for surreptitious tippling on stair-cases.}}

